---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevFormatSql from '@/components/tools/dev/DevFormatSql.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="sql-formatter">
  <DevFormatSql client:load mode="format" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to format a SQL query</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Paste the query, or use <strong>Load .sql file</strong> for a file too big to paste.</li>
      <li>Pick the dialect you run. It sets the keyword list, the comment syntax and the identifier quoting.</li>
      <li>Choose the keyword case and indent width. The output pane updates on each keystroke.</li>
      <li>Copy the result, download it as <code>.sql</code>, or press <strong>Replace input with result</strong> and continue editing.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>What the SQL formatter changes</h2>
    <p>
      It changes only whitespace. It does not rewrite <code>!=</code> to <code>&lt;&gt;</code>, remove the
      <code>AS</code> from aliases, or reflow a <code>CASE</code> expression into a different shape. It
      tokenizes the input, decides where the line breaks and indentation go, and prints each token exactly as
      you wrote it.
    </p>
    <p>The layout rules:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Top-level clauses (<code>SELECT</code>, <code>FROM</code>, <code>WHERE</code>, <code>GROUP BY</code>, <code>HAVING</code>, <code>ORDER BY</code>, <code>LIMIT</code>) start at the left edge of their statement.</li>
      <li>Joins get their own line, with <code>ON</code> on the same line so the join condition reads as one unit.</li>
      <li><code>AND</code> and <code>OR</code> start a continuation line indented one step, so a five-condition <code>WHERE</code> reads as a list.</li>
      <li>Select-list columns go one per line, so a diff shows one added column and not a whole rewritten line.</li>
      <li>Subqueries open a new indent level; function-call parentheses do not, so <code>coalesce(a, b)</code> stays on one line while <code>IN (SELECT …)</code> breaks.</li>
      <li><code>CASE</code> puts each <code>WHEN</code> and the <code>ELSE</code> on its own line and aligns <code>END</code> with the <code>CASE</code>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Why not to format SQL on a server</h2>
    <p>
      A query shows your schema: table names, column names, the shape of your data model. It often also holds
      a literal, such as an account ID, an email address or a date range that identifies a customer. Some
      queries contain connection strings, credentials in a <code>COPY</code> or <code>FDW</code> statement, or
      internal hostnames.
    </p>
    <p>
      In 2025, security researchers enumerated the public &ldquo;saved snippet&rdquo; URLs of several online
      formatting sites and recovered about 80,000 documents. Many contained live cloud keys and database
      connection strings, from users who only clicked Format. This formatter sends no text. Open the panel
      under the tool and check that the request counter stays at zero, or disconnect from the network and
      continue.
    </p>

    <h2>Dialect differences that change the output</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Dialect</th><th>Identifier quoting</th><th>Notable keywords</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>PostgreSQL</td><td><code>"double quotes"</code></td><td>ILIKE, RETURNING, ON CONFLICT, dollar-quoted bodies, <code>::</code> casts</td></tr>
        <tr><td>MySQL / MariaDB</td><td><code>`backticks`</code></td><td>ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE, STRAIGHT_JOIN, <code>#</code> comments, backslash escapes in strings</td></tr>
        <tr><td>SQL Server</td><td><code>[brackets]</code></td><td>TOP, OUTPUT, MERGE, APPLY, <code>@variables</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Oracle</td><td><code>"double quotes"</code></td><td>CONNECT BY, START WITH, MINUS, DUAL</td></tr>
        <tr><td>SQLite</td><td>Any of the above</td><td>PRAGMA, AUTOINCREMENT, upsert syntax</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Snowflake / BigQuery</td><td>Double quotes / backticks</td><td>QUALIFY, UNNEST, LATERAL FLATTEN</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Keeping a team&rsquo;s SQL consistent</h2>
    <p>
      Format the same way each time. Agree on keyword case, indent width, and whether select-list columns go
      one per line, and write the rules down. Uppercase keywords, a two-space indent and one column per line
      is the most common convention in analytics teams, because it gives the smallest diffs. Format before
      review: a reviewer who must reformat a query in their head reads it less carefully.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
